  <question type="stack">
    <name>
      <text>Q1 - Factor Levels</text>
    </name>
    <questiontext format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<h3>Data detective: suspicious categories</h3><p>A summary of the <code>consumptionform</code> column gives the following frequencies.
The responses describe when respondents eat cassava.</p><table class="table table-bordered"><thead><tr><th>Response</th><th>Frequency</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Breakfast, lunch and dinner</td><td>{@a@}</td></tr><tr><td>Lunch, dinner and breakfast</td><td>{@b@}</td></tr><tr><td>Breakfast, dinner and lunch</td><td>{@c@}</td></tr><tr><td>Breakfast only</td><td>{@d@}</td></tr><tr><td>Dinner only</td><td>{@e@}</td></tr></tbody></table><p><em>This is a small teaching extract; the final frequencies are illustrative.</em></p><h4>A. Predict</h4><p>Before doing any calculations, what do you suspect about the first three responses?
[[input:predict]] [[validation:predict]] [[feedback:prtpredict]]</p><h4>B. Investigate</h4><p>How many factor levels are shown in the original summary?
[[input:levels]] [[validation:levels]] [[feedback:prtlevels]]</p><p>How many distinct consumption patterns do these five labels represent, once
equivalent responses have been combined?
[[input:patterns]] [[validation:patterns]] [[feedback:prtpatterns]]</p><p>How many respondents reported eating cassava at breakfast, lunch <em>and</em> dinner?
[[input:combined]] [[validation:combined]] [[feedback:prtcombined]]</p>
<h4>C. Interpret</h4><p>Which corrected frequency table preserves all the information about these
three consumption patterns?
[[input:tablechoice]] [[validation:tablechoice]] [[feedback:prttable]]</p><p>Why is combining the equivalent factor levels useful?
[[input:reason]] [[validation:reason]] [[feedback:prtreason]]</p>]]></text>
    </questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<h3>Worked solution</h3><p>The original summary displays five factor levels, but the first three labels
all describe eating cassava at breakfast, lunch and dinner. Their word order differs,
not their meaning.</p><p>After combining them there are three distinct consumption patterns:
all three meals, breakfast only, and dinner only.</p><p>The corrected frequency for all three meals is
{@a@}+{@b@}+{@c@}={@allthree@}.</p><table class="table table-bordered"><thead><tr><th>Consumption pattern</th><th>Corrected frequency</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Breakfast, lunch and dinner</td><td>{@allthree@}</td></tr><tr><td>Breakfast only</td><td>{@d@}</td></tr><tr><td>Dinner only</td><td>{@e@}</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The total number of responses stays the same:
{@allthree@}+{@d@}+{@e@}={@total@}.
Cleaning the factor levels prevents one consumption pattern from appearing
as several different categories in summaries and graphs.</p>]]></text>
    </generalfeedback>
